# QMD Search for Documentation

This project has 148 markdown docs indexed with QMD (tobi/qmd). Use qmd BEFORE reading files manually - it's faster and finds relevant content across the entire docs/ folder.

## When to Use QMD

- Looking for how a feature works → `qmd search "feature name"`
- Finding API endpoints → `qmd search "endpoint api"`
- Understanding architecture → `qmd search "system architecture"`
- Troubleshooting → `qmd search "error handling"`
- Finding code examples → `qmd search "example python"`

## Commands (in order of speed)

```bash
# 1. FAST keyword search (use first, 90% of the time)
qmd search "canvas pin system" -n 5

# 2. Get full document content after finding it
qmd get "CANVAS_SYSTEM.md" --full

# 3. Semantic search (when keywords don't match)
qmd vsearch "how does jarvis remember things"

# 4. List files in a section
qmd ls jarvis-docs/api
```

## Key Indexed Sections

- `/api` - REST API endpoints (port 8880)
- `/mcp` - MCP server configs
- `/n8n` - Workflow automation
- `/opencode` - Coding agent
- `/service` - systemd services
- `INTELLIGENCE_LAYER.md` - Orchestration brain
- `MEMORY_SYSTEM.md` - Memory architecture
- `TOOL_MANAGEMENT.md` - 54+ tools list

## Tips

- Add `-n 10` for more results
- Use `--full` with `qmd get` to see entire doc
- Search terms are case-insensitive
- Prefer qmd over grep/find for docs - it has rich context annotations

## Why This Matters

Reading docs directly wastes tokens. QMD finds the exact section needed:
- 148 files × ~500 lines avg = 74,000 lines of docs
- QMD search returns just the relevant snippets
- Context annotations improve search relevance
